---
# Settings for the automated migrations, which openDesk runs as part of every deployment: in the
# release `migrations-pre` before all other components and in the release `migrations-post` after
# all of them.
#
# The migrations themselves - which actions run, in which stage, in which order and with which
# parameters, and from which openDesk releases they can be upgraded - are defined by openDesk in
# `helmfile/shared/migrations.yaml.gotmpl` and are not configurable.
migrations:
  # How the deployment treats the two migration jobs themselves. Both settings are deliberately
  # separate from `debug.cleanup.*`, which governs the jobs of all other components: a migration
  # job is not a component that can simply be redeployed, it is a one-time change to your data, and
  # its log is the only record of what it did.
  job:
    # Seconds a deployment waits for a migration job to complete, before it gives up on it and
    # reports the release as failed.
    #
    # The deployment waits on purpose - the components after `migrations-pre`, respectively the
    # deployment as a whole after `migrations-post`, must not continue on a migration that is still
    # running or has failed. How long a migration takes depends on your data, so raise this for a
    # large IAM: a migration that is still working when this elapses keeps running, but the
    # deployment has already been reported as failed and no longer waits for its outcome.
    timeoutSeconds: 3600

    # Seconds a completed migration job and its Pod are kept, so that its log stays readable.
    #
    # The log is where a migration reports what it did, per object, and for the values it replaced
    # it is the only record there is. The default keeps it for a week; `0` keeps it without a time
    # limit.
    #
    # Note that this is an upper bound, not a guarantee: the jobs are deployed as hooks that are
    # replaced on the next deployment, so a job's log survives at most until you deploy again.
    # Anything you need beyond that has to be collected from the Pod, e.g. by your log aggregation.
    keepOutputSeconds: 604800

  # Where the two migration stages hand work over. They are separate jobs, so an action that has to
  # read something before your release is applied and act on it afterwards is two actions, and the
  # first leaves what it worked out in an object store - not in a ConfigMap, which holds 1 MiB and
  # would fail exactly in the installations that have the most to migrate.
  #
  # Which store, bucket and identity that is comes from `objectstores.migrations`, the bucket
  # openDesk provisions for this; the migration jobs reach it under the endpoint its other
  # consumers use, which is your deployment's public one. What is settable here is how they trust
  # and address it, once for every action rather than per action:
  objectStore:
    # Set to the path of a mounted CA certificate when your object store's certificate is issued by
    # a CA the migrations image does not know. Mount it through the migration releases'
    # `extraVolumes`/`extraVolumeMounts`.
    caBundle: ""
    # Only for test deployments with a self-signed certificate. Switching this off means the
    # handover between the two stages travels over a connection nothing authenticates.
    verifySSL: true
    # Seconds a single request to the object store may take.
    requestTimeoutSeconds: 30

  # `actionsSkip` opts out of single migration actions, e.g. when a migration is considered too
  # complex or too risky for this environment.
  #
  # It mirrors the `actions` structure in `helmfile/shared/migrations.yaml.gotmpl`: an entry names
  # the stage (the list it is in), the `id` and the `tag` of the action it skips. Both have to
  # match the declared action exactly, including the absence of a tag, so that an opt-out can never
  # silently suppress a later, different piece of work that reuses the same action under another
  # tag. The action's `runMode` is not part of an opt-out.
  #
  # An entry naming an action of that stage under a different tag stops the deployment before any
  # action runs - it would otherwise let exactly the action through that you excluded. An entry
  # naming an action the stage does not declare at all is only logged as a warning: that is what
  # an opt-out becomes once the release it was written for is behind you.
  #
  # **Warning:** The automated migrations bring your deployment in line with the openDesk release
  # you are deploying. Skipping an action means the migration it implements is not applied and the
  # affected component may stay on the old state, so you take over the responsibility for its
  # outcome - for example because you already performed the step manually or because you need to
  # perform it in a maintenance window of your own.
  #
  # Example:
  # ```
  # migrations:
  #   actionsSkip:
  #     pre:
  #       - id: "ox_functional_accounts_export"
  #         tag: "v1.18.0"
  #     post:
  #       - id: "ox_shared_accounts_import"
  #         tag: "v1.18.0"
  # ```
  actionsSkip:
    # Actions to be skipped in the stage `migrations-pre`.
    pre: []
    # Actions to be skipped in the stage `migrations-post`.
    post: []
  # Various options for specific actions.
  #
  # Every action applies its change, and it is not configurable whether it does: the actions of a
  # release bring your deployment in line with that release, and the components deployed after them
  # rely on what they wrote. Where an action needs a decision that cannot be derived from your
  # data, it is asked for here.
  #
  # What each action does is documented in `docs/migrations-automated.md`. To take the
  # responsibility for a migration yourself, opt out of its actions through `actionsSkip` above.
  actionOptions:
    # The migration of the OX App Suite Functional Accounts still in your IAM to the Shared
    # Accounts that replace them, in two halves: `ox_functional_accounts_export` records and
    # deletes them in `migrations-pre`, `ox_shared_accounts_import` creates the Shared Accounts in
    # `migrations-post`.
    #
    # To migrate them yourself instead, opt out through `actionsSkip` above - of *both* actions, so
    # in `pre` *and* in `post`, as opting out of only one leaves the migration half applied.
    oxFunctionalToSharedAccounts:
      # The permission every user of a migrated account is linked with, given as the `name` of the
      # `oxmail/shared_account_permission` object - not its display name, which is a label that may
      # be reworded. Defaults to openDesk's "Mail: Full access, Calendar: Full access", which comes
      # closest to what a Functional Account granted its users; `ox_shared_accounts_import` reports
      # the permissions your deployment provides should this one not exist.
      permission: "opendesk_mail_author_calendar_author"
...
